{
  "image_name": "2b427c88d3ec248840f0df86961f1f21.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the VEGF/VEGFR2 signaling axis and where anti-angiogenic drugs can intervene, while also comparing how selective those drugs are. On the left, VEGF binds the VEGFR2 receptor at the plasma membrane, activating the receptor’s intracellular tyrosine kinase domains. This triggers two main downstream pathways: a RAS–MEK–ERK cascade and a PI3K–AKT–mTOR cascade, both shown as vertical signaling routes beneath the receptor.\n\nThe center and right columns map drug classes onto this pathway and indicate their specificity. Agents acting at the ligand or receptor level include anti-VEGF drugs, soluble receptors, and anti-VEGFR drugs. These appear relatively more selective, with anti-VEGF labeled monospecific and the receptor-directed approaches affecting about 2–3 targets. In contrast, drugs aimed at intracellular signaling—tyrosine kinase inhibitors, PI3K inhibitors, AKT inhibitors, and ERK/mTOR inhibitors—are labeled as having multiple targets.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a tradeoff between where therapy acts in the VEGF pathway and how specific it is: the closer the intervention is to VEGF itself, the narrower the targeting appears; deeper signaling inhibitors seem broader and less specific.",
